WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:03.461
At some point growing up, 
my vision changed.

00:00:03.628 --> 00:00:07.340
And slowly I stopped being able 
to see past about...

00:00:08.258 --> 00:00:10.343
this far in front of my face.

00:00:10.719 --> 00:00:12.971
Basically, anything past...

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:15.432
like 10 inches in front of my eyes...

00:00:16.391 --> 00:00:17.183
is blurry.

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:19.185
So eventually I got glasses 

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:22.939
and with them my world 
turns from this...

00:00:24.274 --> 00:00:25.066
to this.

00:00:27.402 --> 00:00:29.529
This whole experience

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:31.531
as inconvenient as it is...

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.784
is more widespread than it's ever been.

00:00:35.118 --> 00:00:36.119
[overlapping] “-Myopia.” 
“-Myopia.”

00:00:36.119 --> 00:00:39.289
[overlapping] “- Myopia, am I saying it right?”
“-A rise in short-sightedness...”

00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:41.624
“...and the researchers actually 
called it an epidemic...”

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:44.002
“...but they're still trying to figure out 
why this is...”

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:46.838
Rates of myopia or 
near-sightedness

00:00:46.838 --> 00:00:49.132
or needing glasses to 
see things far away...

00:00:49.424 --> 00:00:51.342
have been rising for decades.

00:00:51.342 --> 00:00:52.510
In the US, where I live

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.555
just 25% of people
were myopic in 1971.

00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:58.725
By 2004, that number 
was up to 42%.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:00:59.934
And if current trends continue

00:00:59.934 --> 00:01:02.062
it’s estimated that half of 
the world's population

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:03.688
will be myopic by 2050.

00:01:03.938 --> 00:01:07.192
In Hong Kong, Singapore, 
Taiwan and South Korea

00:01:07.192 --> 00:01:08.735
rates are already a lot 
higher than that.

00:01:08.818 --> 00:01:10.987
And a growing portion 
have high myopia

00:01:10.987 --> 00:01:14.949
which the WHO categorizes as 
a prescription stronger than -5.

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:17.619
That puts them at risk of 
losing their sight one day.

00:01:17.994 --> 00:01:19.579
For decades, 
researchers thought 

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:20.955
that whether or not 
you needed glasses

00:01:20.955 --> 00:01:22.832
was just a matter 
of genetics.

00:01:22.832 --> 00:01:24.250
And it partly is.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.294
Having one myopic parent 
doubles your odds

00:01:26.294 --> 00:01:27.128
of being nearsighted

00:01:27.128 --> 00:01:29.756
and having 2 increases 
the odds 5 fold.

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:32.467
But human genetics 
don't change this fast.

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:35.345
The abruptness of this increase 
suggests that

00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:37.388
that this change 
is environmental.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:39.265
Something about the way 
we live today

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:40.683
is making it harder 
and harder

00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:42.769
for people to see 
at a distance.

00:01:43.144 --> 00:01:43.812
So what could it be?

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:49.609
Most people are born

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:51.861
with eyes that are too short 
from front to back.

00:01:52.237 --> 00:01:55.198
In this shape, the lens focuses 
images behind the retina.

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:57.534
That's the light sensitive tissue 
at the back of the eye.

00:01:57.784 --> 00:02:00.203
That makes the eye hyperopic 
or farsighted.

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.580
Blurry up close and 
clear from far away.

00:02:03.039 --> 00:02:05.083
But as we grow up, 
our eyes grow too.

00:02:05.166 --> 00:02:07.252
Until they reach a spherical shape.

00:02:07.252 --> 00:02:09.379
In this shape, the lens 
focuses light directly

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:11.798
onto the retina and produces 
a clear image.

00:02:12.215 --> 00:02:14.342
But sometimes the eye 
keeps growing longer.

00:02:14.592 --> 00:02:17.804
In this shape, the lens can focus 
up close images onto the retina.

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:20.181
But at a distance, images 
focus at a point

00:02:20.181 --> 00:02:21.558
in front of the retina...

00:02:21.724 --> 00:02:23.309
making distance vision blurry.

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.186
So all of us with myopia

00:02:25.186 --> 00:02:27.480
just have eyeballs that 
have grown too long.

00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:30.859
The eye does not look like 
a basketball anymore.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:33.486
It looks more like a rugby ball.

00:02:34.028 --> 00:02:35.155
That's Seang Mei Saw.

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:38.366
She's a myopia epidemiologist 
and physician in Singapore.

00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:40.326
It is a lifelong disease

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:43.246
so once you're myopic, 
it doesn't regress.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:45.582
So what's making more and more
people’s eyes

00:02:45.582 --> 00:02:46.875
grow longer than they should?

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:48.293
The evidence points to the way

00:02:48.293 --> 00:02:50.587
we spend time in childhood 
and adolescence.

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:52.505
That's when our eyes 
grow fastest.

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:54.883
So it's when most people's 
myopia develops

00:02:54.883 --> 00:02:56.092
and then stabilizes.

00:02:56.092 --> 00:02:57.302
Though it can develop later

00:02:57.302 --> 00:02:58.845
if you abuse your vision enough.

00:02:58.928 --> 00:03:01.181
Two factors in particular 
have the biggest influence.

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:04.350
Near work or the time that we spend 
looking at things up close...

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:06.561
and how much time we spend indoors.

00:03:06.644 --> 00:03:09.105
In a healthy eye, muscles have to 
squeeze the lens

00:03:09.105 --> 00:03:11.649
in order to focus up close images 
onto the retina.

00:03:11.774 --> 00:03:13.985
So some experts theorize that 
if your eyes grow up

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:15.570
straining to look at 
things up close

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.405
a lot of the time, they'll 
just grow longer

00:03:17.405 --> 00:03:18.865
to reduce that strain.

00:03:19.115 --> 00:03:21.117
But the evidence on this explanation 
is mixed.

00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:23.828
The stronger explanation 
is time spent indoors.

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:26.581
Exposure to bright 
outdoor light

00:03:26.581 --> 00:03:28.958
stimulates the production of 
dopamine in the retina.

00:03:28.958 --> 00:03:31.377
This neurotransmitter regulates 
the eyes growth...

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:32.712
without enough dopamine

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:34.839
the eye doesn't know when 
to stop growing

00:03:34.839 --> 00:03:37.091
and indoors it's hard 
to get enough.

00:03:37.091 --> 00:03:38.676
The light from the sun

00:03:38.676 --> 00:03:41.512
has up to a 100,000 lux 
on a sunny day.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:44.682
Whereas in the room 
the light levels generally

00:03:44.682 --> 00:03:47.101
and only about 200 to 300 lux.

00:03:47.268 --> 00:03:50.647
But between electronic devices
and early emphasis on academics...

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:52.732
eye experts believe that children today

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:55.276
are growing up with a combination 
of too little daylight

00:03:55.276 --> 00:03:57.403
and too much time doing 
things up close.

00:03:57.946 --> 00:03:59.280
And nowhere is that more apparent

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.199
than in East and Southeast Asia.

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:04.118
Children in Asia are not spending 
that amount of time outside.

00:04:04.285 --> 00:04:07.121
This could possibly be because 
of the education system

00:04:07.121 --> 00:04:09.290
has become much more competitive.

00:04:09.791 --> 00:04:11.542
The children have a lot more work.

00:04:11.793 --> 00:04:13.419
They attend teaching centers

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.964
and you spend more time 
reading and writing.

00:04:16.005 --> 00:04:18.549
Needing glasses or contacts 
to see for the rest of your life

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:20.051
is obviously inconvenient.

00:04:20.301 --> 00:04:21.177
But in the long term

00:04:21.177 --> 00:04:23.471
the consequences of that 
distorted eyeball shape

00:04:23.471 --> 00:04:24.639
can become serious.

00:04:25.056 --> 00:04:27.225
University of Houston 
Professor of Optometry 

00:04:27.225 --> 00:04:28.893
Mark Bullimore, explained 
this to me.

00:04:29.227 --> 00:04:31.604
You know, you're born with a 
finite amount of tissue

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:33.940
that make up the various coats 
of your eyeball.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.901
Excessive elongation of that 
quite simply

00:04:36.901 --> 00:04:39.612
places additional stress 
on those structures.

00:04:39.696 --> 00:04:42.782
The retina has been stretched 
so much that starts to break

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:46.411
and then sort of peel off like 
an old piece of paint.

00:04:46.869 --> 00:04:48.955
The longer those eye structures 
are stretched

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:50.290
the higher the risk 
of disorders

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:53.418
like myopic macular degeneration, 
retinal detachment...

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:55.461
glaucoma and cataracts.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:58.965
So we're finding this almost 
linear relationship between them.

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:02.051
The amount of myopia and 
the risks to your vision...

00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:03.511
later in life.

00:05:03.511 --> 00:05:07.765
We used to think about myopia 
as an optical defect.

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:10.518
Now we think about it 
much more as a disease.

00:05:10.852 --> 00:05:12.729
And the earlier a child 
becomes myopic

00:05:12.729 --> 00:05:15.023
the more serious their myopia 
can become

00:05:15.023 --> 00:05:17.442
and the greater the risk 
of debilitating conditions.

00:05:17.525 --> 00:05:20.737
Which means it's important to 
intervene as early as possible.

00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:21.946
So, what does that look like?

00:05:23.114 --> 00:05:25.074
For those who start to develop myopia

00:05:25.074 --> 00:05:26.117
there's treatment.

00:05:26.117 --> 00:05:29.412
First are multifocal, 
soft contacts, and glasses.

00:05:29.412 --> 00:05:31.789
They make peripheral vision 
intentionally blurry

00:05:31.789 --> 00:05:34.334
which appears to slow the progression 
of myopia.

00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:37.754
Then there's orthokeratology 
or ortho-k lenses...

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.048
hard contact lenses worn 
only at night

00:05:40.048 --> 00:05:42.342
that reshape the wearer's cornea 
while they sleep...

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:44.802
so that they can see at a distance 
during the day.

00:05:44.802 --> 00:05:46.721
And there are atropine eye drops 

00:05:46.721 --> 00:05:49.599
low doses of a substance that
 temporarily paralyzes

00:05:49.599 --> 00:05:51.267
the eyes’ focusing muscles

00:05:51.267 --> 00:05:53.478
which seems to reduce 
the development of myopia.

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:55.980
But the first line of defense 
is prevention.

00:05:56.397 --> 00:05:58.941
The simplest and most effective way 
to prevent myopia

00:05:58.941 --> 00:06:01.444
is to get kids to spend 
more time outside.

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:03.196
In Taiwan, the government introduced

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:06.032
a nationwide program in 2010, 
encouraging schools

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:08.743
to get students outside for 
2 hours every day.

00:06:08.993 --> 00:06:11.371
It appears to have 
successfully reversed

00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:13.289
 a 40 year-long increase 
in myopia rates.

00:06:13.289 --> 00:06:14.415
And since 2001

00:06:14.415 --> 00:06:17.710
Singapore has funded public education
promoting time outdoors...

00:06:18.002 --> 00:06:20.505
and conducted annual 
vision screenings at schools.

00:06:20.671 --> 00:06:22.090
And it seems 
to be working.

00:06:22.632 --> 00:06:25.093
Right now, these rates may be 
higher than ever

00:06:25.093 --> 00:06:27.845
but the future of myopia 
will only look like this

00:06:27.845 --> 00:06:29.931
if we keep doing things 
the same way.

00:06:30.473 --> 00:06:32.558
And we've never been in a better position

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:33.684
to change.

